{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's error-type symmetry argument defines 'reliability gain' as a reduction in error variance and an increase in auditability, not as a raw increase in objectively verifiable outcomes. The problem asks to identify evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes a genuine reliability gain (higher accuracy) from mere checklist compliance. By shifting the failure mode to commission errors and relying on external audit (which is explicitly noted as a countercondition that could be unreliable), it conflates 'legible compliance' with 'reliability' without providing a single-evaluation metric to prove the panel achieves more correct answers.","target":"2926716af499ee7bb95a2159dbf0ddaae787b5a0c3ca9544b83b566924a40217"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target asserts a reliability gain based on 'error-type symmetry' but fails to identify what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish this from mere checklist compliance. By shifting the burden to an external 'audit' process, it avoids providing a single-evaluation metric. If the audit process is as unreliable as the expert's intuition (as noted in its own counterconditions), the target has no internal mechanism to prove the panel's 'commission' errors are actually caught or that the raw accuracy is higher.","target":"48a9c104fccc01e2c2b95c7f92897b1e5e2fe0d03fa59fa7fb2fbfbfd21b216e"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target provides no specific result as its content is cut off and missing. Without a defined claim or mechanism, it fails to address the problem of arguing for or against the panel's reliability or identifying the evidence to distinguish genuine gain from compliance.","target":"67696f717179c35329c77ccb118e6025f04e9a2fac6ff61149b486c775e048ce"},{"attack":false,"case":"Assuming the fourth target (SRC_004) was omitted due to truncation, it is marked as not attacked.","target":"8d5bb22c568ccb7473fc4a445e03be8c460902c81764a7d42e1b818f0f5416a6"}]}